  "text": "קובע\nהחלטון\n\nעושות\nעושה\n.squareup\n.square\n\n.strictEqual\n.strictEqual\n\n判断题\n\n재판매\n재판매\n. The pyramid is constructed with five positive integers on its faces, and these integers will be used to construct the edges, where each edge is labeled with the sum of the two numbers on the adjacent faces.\n\nLet’s denote these five positive integers as \\(a, b, c, d, e\\), and the edges are labeled as \\(x, y, z\\), \\(y+w, z+v, x+w\\), and they alternate according to one face's number (with edge \\(x\\)) being added with the lone different number \\(b\\) (and could be simplified further with a few more additional equations if proved in the process).\n\nThe condition says the product of the edges sums is 243, i.e., \\(x \\cdot y \\cdot z \\cdot (y + w) \\cdot (z + v) \\cdot (x + w) = 243\\).\n\n243 simplifies to \\(3^5\\).\n\nWe need to investigate the structure of these integers \\(a, b, c, d, e\\) and match them against sum products to match 243. Only some integers like 3, 9, 27, etc., can be a right fit since exponents of 3 can be used.\n\nLet's explore the face labels and how the sums are derived in a configuration consistent with a pyramid's design.\n\n%1:x=9,y=3,z=1\nx = 9, y = 3, z = 1 (n3_m7)\nx = 3, y = 9, z = 3\n3 is the only prime factorization of 3 here, changing its assumptions is needed\ny = 3, x = 9, again (double the \"edges\" smeared in configurations),\nx = 3, y = 9, z = 27 (out-of-range),\ny=z=9\nx+z=12; output does not match sum for a constant 9\nKύα너\nKử\nKύろ\nKύ.*;\nύ3 < ȿό\n\n艅",
